  "text": {
    "work": "work",
    "card": "Index three members through orthogonal grooves between offset shafts.",
    "oldham": "Oldham coupling",
    "offset_coupling": "offset shaft coupling",
    "wrong_phase": "Choose the adjacent turn matching the current phase.",
    "free_hand": "Free a hand to turn the rear blue input shaft control.",
    "howto_turn": "Use the next or previous offer matching phase at the rear blue input control. Blue I is the original input hub and shaft, orange O the original output hub and shaft, and green M ONE rigid middle disk with opposite-face keys at ninety degrees. Two fixed parallel shaft axes are sixty millimeters apart. Input and output machined floor-and-rail bars have actual open face grooves at ninety degrees to each other, not solid blocks painted as slots. Both original keys stay engaged in their grooves at every indexed endpoint. All three rigid members turn by the same positive angle, counterclockwise from the front, in sixteen 22.5 degree steps. The whole middle disk also translates to rounded C=30(1+cos(2theta),sin(2theta)) millimeters relative to the input axis: a thirty millimeter circle traversed twice per shaft turn. Pale member indices begin along+X; mounted red input/output marks identify their initial angles, while I/M/O identities stay fixed. Both shafts remain in hollow bearing sleeves joined to the original support frame. Natural layering hides parts of the two sliding joints from a single view: inspect from the side or rear without detaching parts. No connecting rod or cam is present. This is indexed geometry only: no continuous path/contact, force, torque, friction, power or load transmission, speed, autonomous timer, output item, loose custody, security or engine collision/support change is simulated."
  },
